## 3. SCHEMA CHANGES

Read `prisma/schema.prisma` first. Only add what's missing.

```prisma
model Drive {
  // ... all existing fields unchanged ...

  // Make department ownership optional — a null departmentId means
  // this drive was posted centrally by a Super Admin, not by a
  // department admin.
  departmentId String?   // CHANGE: was required, now optional
  department   Department? @relation(fields: [departmentId], references: [id])

  // Track who actually created the drive (dept admin OR super admin),
  // for audit/display purposes. Distinct from departmentId ownership.
  createdByUserId String?
  createdByUser   User?   @relation("DriveCreatedBy", fields: [createdByUserId], references: [id])

  // Explicit flag rather than relying on departmentId === null everywhere —
  // makes queries and UI branching clearer and safer.
  isCentralDrive Boolean @default(false)
}
```

> Check existing relations on `User` and `Department` before adding the new relation name — avoid duplicate relation names or ambiguous relation errors. Run `npx prisma format` after editing.

Run one migration for this unit: `npx prisma migrate dev --name central-drives`.

**Backward compatibility check (mandatory):** every existing query that does `where: { departmentId: department.id }` (e.g. `getAdminDrives`, drive ownership checks in `updateDrive`) must continue to work unchanged for department-owned drives — `departmentId` being nullable does not change behavior for rows where it's set. Re-run `npx tsc --noEmit` after the schema change to catch every place TypeScript now flags `departmentId` as possibly null and fix each one deliberately (don't blanket-cast with `!`).

